{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Let Dan be.</b> Scripture compared him to a serpent for several reasons. The first is that Samson did not fight against his enemies like other kings or judges with large armies, but alone he would go out against them suddenly, like a viper emerging from its hole. The second is that just as the serpent receives a charm, so Samson received a charm, as it is said (Judges 16:16–17): ״And it came to pass, when she pressed him daily with her words and urged him, that his soul was vexed to death, and he told her all his heart,״ and it is written (ibid.): ״If I am shaven, then my strength will go from me and I will become weak and be like any other man.״ Furthermore, just as the serpent is called שרף (a burning one) because its venom burns, so Samson burned with fire the standing grain of the Philistines, as it is written (Judges 15:5): ״And he kindled fire in torches and sent them into the standing grain of the Philistines, and burned up from the shock to the standing grain.״ And just as the serpent has no legs, so was Samson חגר בשתי רגליו (bound in his two legs). And just as the serpent strikes only in a low place, because of the curse of the primordial serpent — ״upon your belly you shall go״ — and it is written (Genesis 3:15): ״and you shall strike his heel,״ so Samson bit the heels of the horse, an allusion to the two pillars upon which the house rested, and it says: ״and his rider shall fall backward״ — regarding the three thousand who were on the roof. And he called them ״the rider who falls backward,״ because when the serpent bites the horse's heels, the horse raises its head and its forelegs and its rider falls backward. And just as the serpent strikes without a sword, so those slain by Samson were without a sword, as it is said (Judges 16:30): ״And the dead whom he killed at his death were more than those whom he killed in his lifetime.״ And you will find in Bereshit Rabbah: just as the serpent takes vengeance, so Samson took vengeance, as it is said (ibid.): ״Let me take vengeance for one of my two eyes from the Philistines,״ end quote." "<b>And know</b> that the meaning is explicit in Dan, and what was alluded to in Reuben was spelled out in Dan, and this is the sense of ידין עמו and likewise נחש עלי דרך, and this is clear." "<b>A basilisk on the path.</b> The smallest species among serpents is called שפיפון. <b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״A basilisk on the path״ — Samson was חגר בשתי רגליו (bound in his two legs), for thus it is written concerning Balaam (Numbers 22:25–26): ״and she crushed Balaam's foot against the wall,״ and it is written (v. 23): ״וילך שפי,״ and here it is written שפיפון — he was bound in his two legs."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:42.649401718Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}